    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "**Results:** This paper performed a comparative analysis of two classifiers in two different data window sizes (1-minute, 3-minute) for all three subjects (S2–S4) of stress states derived from chest sensor data (ECG, EDA, TEMP, RESP) using ARIMA and Random Forest classifiers. Random Forest consistently outperformed ARIMA overall — mean accuracy was 0.76 vs. 0.53 (1-min) and 0.78 vs. 0.54 (3-min), with an even wider F1 gap (0.74 vs. 0.31 and 0.76 vs. 0.43). There was a wide range of this gap across signals. However, ARIMA fared poorly in the ECG and RESP datasets, and its ROC curves yielded AUC near or below 0.5 (random), indicating that the linear model used by ARIMA is not valid for these signals for capturing their stress-sensitive behavior. The only time that ARIMA outperformed Random Forest was during the 3-minute window for S2 (Random Forest F1 was 0.6799, while ARIMA's was 0.6840). The most erratic signal was EDA, a signal that went from near-perfect (S4) to near-zero (S2), which was probably a pipeline problem and not necessarily a model issue. The length of the windows did not help both models, but more so for ARIMA as its average F1 improved on the order of 13 points with the increase in window length from 1 minute to 3 minutes, corroborating the findings of its improved ECG forecasting accuracy at longer windows.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Implications:** This does not confirm the potential of ARIMA in every situation, as for the ECG and respiration signals, the added cost of Random Forest is clearly warranted because they are fast and complex signals. However, for more slowly changing signals, such as temperature, ARIMA was close enough (and briefly better) that, in real-time, resource-constrained wearable situations, it might be the more practical option because it is simpler and less computation intensive. The choice of model is not necessarily one-size-fits-all, but rather model choice may be more appropriately signal-dependent. Because of the small sample size (3 out of 15 subjects available in WESAD) and the simplicity of the classification rule for ARIMA, which often seemed to \"stabilize\" at the majority class, these results are to be interpreted with some care, and future studies should investigate a more sophisticated decision threshold that could meaningfully improve the performance of ARIMA."
